    "available_at": "2026-11-23T16:39:25.650999+00:00",
    "body": "Photo: Liège Airport\nBy Alex Lennane\n23 November 2026\nAirlines have rescheduled flights and moved aircraft to airports in neighbouring countries after a Belgian air traffic control strike disrupted cargo operations at Liège and Brussels, four days before Black Friday.\nLiège Airport spokesperson Christian Delcourt told The Loadstar some 70 flights were affected. The airport has yet to say how many were cancelled, retimed, or rerouted, or when it expects affected freight to move.\nFlight-tracking data showed Emirates SkyCargo flight EK9989 from Dubai, originally due at Liège, was rerouted to Copenhagen. Maastricht Aachen Airport said it expected to take two cargo flights diverted because of the Belgian strike, but did not identify the airlines or the airports the flights were originally due to serve.\nBrussels Airport cancelled all arrivals and departures scheduled between 7am and 9pm local time today, and posted the same notice on its cargo arrivals and departures pages, and advised customers with flights scheduled after 9pm to check with their airlines.\nIt has yet to publish a freighter tally or a timetable for normal cargo operations to resume.\nToday’s walkout, part of a national protest against government austerity measures, is the 11th strike to affect Belgian airspace in 11 months, according to the Board of Airline Representatives in Belgium. Brussels Airlines said it only learned late on Saturday afternoon that controllers would take part, leaving little time to rework Monday’s schedules.\nA stoppage on 2 June affected 35 to 40 flights at Liège, with some diverted to Cologne, local broadcaster Qu4tre reported. Liège has yet to identify the airports taking rerouted flights this time.\nElsewhere, Charleroi cancelled flights for the whole day, while Ostend-Bruges is working with airlines to reschedule flights, including three cargo departures and two arrivals.\nFedEx warned of possible delays to shipments to, from and within Belgium, and Jersey Post Global Logistics also warned that disruption at Brussels Airport could delay inbound international mail and parcels, while the wider strike could affect collections, processing and deliveries across the country.\nChronopost warned that expected blockages on major roads and in industrial areas could lengthen transit times for shipments to and from Belgium throughout Black Friday week. It said all its Belgian sites would remain open, although collections and deliveries would be disrupted.\nThe recent US air traffic control outage showed how long capacity can take to recover once flights resume. The FAA lifted its nationwide ground stop on 13 November, after less than five hours, but capacity on international flights out of the US was still about 10% below the previous week’s level over the weekend, according to WorldACD. Expeditors warned that recovery would vary by airline and airport as carriers repositioned aircraft and crews.\nThe strike falls in one of Liège’s busiest periods for freight, as the airport adjusts to a sharp drop in ecommerce traffic. Liège said small-parcel volumes had fallen 42% after the EU’s €3 duty took effect on 1 July, reducing the number of dedicated ecommerce flights. Aircraft movements fell 6% year on year in the third quarter, while freight tonnage was virtually flat, at 334,467 tonnes.\nStronger exports helped lift total throughput 7.3% year on year, to 1.03m tonnes in the first nine months. Liège singled out pharmaceuticals, data centre equipment, and flowers as supporting July volumes.\nLast month it signed an agreement with Brazil’s Viracopos Airport to attract freighter services between Belgium and Brazil.\nCheck out today’s News in Brief podcast , on rates, routes and One Record.",
